    ext('ferritin-transferrin','Anemia','Simultaneously decreased plasma ferritin and increased plasma transferrin are best explained by', ['Impaired liver protein synthesis','Inflammation','Iron deficiency','Hemolysis'],[2],[217],'Low ferritin with raised transferrin/TIBC favors depleted iron stores. Inflammatory iron restriction usually gives normal/high ferritin instead.',['iron','iron-nih'])
    ext('pt-use','Coagulation','Prothrombin time can help assess', ['Platelet number and function','The extrinsic (tissue-factor) coagulation pathway','The intrinsic coagulation pathway','Liver synthetic function'],[1,3],[219],'PT assesses extrinsic/common coagulation pathways and can reflect liver synthetic impairment; it does not measure platelet function.',['pt','liver-tests'])
    ext('coombs-use','Anemia','The Coombs test is used to detect', ['Antibodies against erythrocytes','Bleeding time','Vitamin B12 absorption','Coagulation-factor concentrations'],[0],[226],'Direct testing detects antibody/complement on red cells; indirect testing detects relevant antibodies in serum. It is not a clotting-time assay.',['aiha'])
    ext('hereditary-bleeding','Coagulation','Which option names common hereditary hypocoagulation disorders? (Choose one best answer.)', ['Factor V Leiden mutation','Hemophilia A and von Willebrand disease','Thrombocytopenia due to hypersplenism','Cirrhosis-related coagulopathy','Leukemia-associated thrombocytopenia'],[1],[229],'Hemophilia and VWD are inherited bleeding disorders. Factor V Leiden increases thrombosis risk; the other listed processes are acquired.',['hemophilia','vwd','fvl'])
    ext('hepcidin-chronic-anemia','Anemia','Altered iron metabolism in anemia of chronic disease is caused by which two mechanisms?', ['Increased hepatic hepcidin production','Accelerated iron transfer from hepatocytes into bile','Reduced iron export from macrophages','Insufficient dietary iron'],[0,2],[230],'Inflammation increases hepcidin and restricts release of stored iron, reducing its availability for erythropoiesis.',['iron-nih'])
    ext('hemophilia-unaffected-parents','Coagulation','Can a boy have hemophilia when neither parent has clinical manifestations?', ['No, because the disorder is hereditary','Yes, because severity increases in every generation','Yes, because a new mutation can occur','Yes, because his mother may be a heterozygous carrier without symptoms','No, because both parents appear healthy'],[2,3],[237],'An asymptomatic carrier mother can transmit an X-linked variant; sporadic/de novo cases also occur. Unaffected parents do not exclude hemophilia.',['hemophilia','hemophilia-genetics'])
